# AGM v1.2.0 — Compact Reference

AGM is a line-oriented format for agent-consumable knowledge graphs. Each file
contains a header followed by one or more node blocks. Nodes are the atomic
knowledge units — independently addressable, typed, and progressively
expandable. An agent should often decide relevance from the `summary` alone.

---

## File Structure

```
agm: 1.0
package: <namespace>      # required; dot-separated, lowercase
version: 0.1.0            # required; semver

node <id>                 # starts a node block; must be unique in package
type: <node-type>         # required
summary: <one line>       # required; loaded in every mode
```

---

## Node Types

| Type | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `facts` | Factual assertions about the domain |
| `rules` | Normative constraints (MUST / MUST NOT) |
| `workflow` | Step-by-step procedural knowledge |
| `entity` | Data model with typed fields |
| `decision` | Architecture or design decision with rationale |
| `exception` | Known error condition and resolution |
| `example` | Illustrative instance |
| `glossary` | Term definition |
| `anti_pattern` | What NOT to do and why |
| `orchestration` | Multi-agent execution plan with parallel groups |
| `ticket` | Actionable work item |

---

## Grammar Summary

### Required fields

```
node <id>         # node identity; dot-separated lowercase
type: <type>      # one of the node types above
summary: <text>   # one-line description
```

### Relationship fields

```
depends: [<id>, ...]       # hard dependencies (DAG enforced)
related_to: [<id>, ...]    # soft associations
replaces: [<id>]           # supersedes older node
conflicts: [<id>, ...]     # incompatible nodes
see_also: [<id>, ...]      # additional references
```

### Operational fields

```
items:                     # unordered list
  - item_one
steps:                     # ordered steps
  - step text
fields:                    # entity fields with types
  field_name: type
input: value
output: value
```

### Explanatory fields

```
detail:
  Multi-line block. Two-space indent.
rationale: Why the decision was made.
tradeoffs:
  - pro: benefit
    con: cost
resolution: How to handle the exception.
notes: Free-form notes.
```

### Executable fields

```
code:
  target: src/path/file.ext
  action: create | append | patch | delete
  lang: rust | python | typescript | ...
  content: |
    <source code>
verify:
  - check: description of what is verified
    cmd: shell command
    expect: expected output fragment
agent_context:
  nodes: [<id>, ...]
  files: [<path>, ...]
  system_hint: runtime hint text
```

### Control fields

```
priority: critical | high | normal | low
stability: high | medium | low | volatile
execution_status: pending | ready | in_progress | completed | failed | blocked | skipped
```

### Multi-value syntax variants

```
# Inline list
tags: [auth, api]

# Block list
items:
  - first
  - second

# Multi-line block
detail:
  Paragraph one.

  Paragraph two.
```

---

## Loading Modes

| Mode | Fields Loaded |
|------|--------------|
| `summary` | `node`, `type`, `summary`, `priority`, `stability` |
| `operational` | + `items`, `steps`, `fields`, `input`, `output`, `depends` |
| `executable` | + `code`, `verify`, `agent_context`, `target` |
| `full` | all fields |

---

## Example: Rules Node

```agm
agm: 1.0
package: auth.platform
version: 0.2.0

node auth.rule.no_secrets_in_browser
type: rules
summary: never expose secrets or tokens to browser-side code
priority: critical
stability: high
items:
  - secrets_never_in_js_bundles
  - api_keys_server_side_only
  - tokens_httponly_cookies_only
rationale: Browser environment is untrusted; any secret exposed can be exfiltrated.
```

---

## Example: Workflow Node

```agm
agm: 1.0
package: deploy.pipeline
version: 1.0.0

node deploy.workflow.release
type: workflow
summary: standard release workflow for production deployments
priority: high
stability: high
depends: [deploy.decision.feature_flags, deploy.facts.environments]
steps:
  - run integration test suite
  - build release artifact with version tag
  - deploy to staging and run smoke tests
  - enable feature flag for 5% of traffic
  - monitor error rate for 30 minutes
  - promote to 100% traffic or rollback
notes: Rollback is automatic if error rate exceeds 1% within the first 15 minutes.
```

---

## Example: Ticket Node

```agm
agm: 1.0
package: backend.tasks
version: 0.1.0

node backend.ticket.add_rate_limit
type: ticket
summary: add per-user rate limiting to the public API endpoints
priority: high
stability: high
depends: [backend.facts.api_endpoints, backend.rules.auth]
detail:
  Implement token-bucket rate limiting at the API gateway layer.
  Limit: 100 requests per minute per authenticated user.
  Unauthenticated requests share a global bucket of 20 req/min.
```
